 looked at me and she  said do you realize that at MIT you can  combine your electrical engineering with  management study and actually try and  create that and okay the thing about  Margaret Daniels Tyler at the time she  was the first black woman to be an  admissions director at any like ivy  league level school she had a presence a  confidence that she just exuded and I  just SAT there and I was like I don't  know why I'm not doing this and so I  decided to apply to the salon school at  her encouragement she was there she  coached me through how to write the  application how to present my  achievements because remember deep down  inside I was still that broken isolated  stupid failure so it would be very hard  for me to share that  and what was great is not only did she  do that during the application process  but she stayed around while I was a  student there while I was doing my  Master's research and one day I came to  her nice like I don't know whether to  apply for this scholarship I had half a  merit scholarship but I was self-funding  my tuition so the other there was  another opportunity through digital  equipment corporation who remembers  digital equipment corporation okay they  were a competitor of IBM's way back when  that no longer exists their computer  still exists in some research labs out  there you could still find deck faxes  and and other many computers they were  actually used a lot in scientific  research but but that doesn't really  matter so they were offering digital  equipment was offering to merit  scholarships to women in management at  MIT and at the time I eighteen percent  or 33 of the 181 students at MIT were  women four percent were under the age of  23 I was 21 all of my classmates were  like you don't have a chance you've  never worked you've never you know you  don't have the experience blah blah blah  blah blah well okay filtering it with  the broken isolated stupid failure  filter I was like okay I don't know that  I should apply for this I mean it seems  sort of and she her simple question was  why not what do you have to lose as like  an entire weekend of writing application  for this thing and she's like yeah but  what do you have to potentially gain I  was like well the fact  I could eat and have a winter coat  Boston later that year because I had  enough money to pay rent and groceries  and stuff so it was kind of a motivating  factor and so i went ahead and i applied  and ironically i did get that  scholarship and i owe much of what has  transpired since then to the time that  Margaret spent with me and the  encouragement she gave to me and her  helping me see what was possible so  Margaret went on to leave MIT after  being a Dean of the Graduate School she  went on to be a vice president of the  United Negro College Fund she also ran  the strategic direction for a little two  billion dollar bill and melinda gates  foundation millennial Scholars Program  she was a phenomenal and still is a  phenomenal person but the thing that she  did so well was mentor people that she  felt had a strength of character and  would work hard to get what they wanted  and work for others so why does  mentoring matter in our communities I'm  actually gonna grab some water I think  about that for a second how many of you  have actually been mentored by somebody  at some point in your career or academic  life how many haven't been a mentor okay  so we could have meant more mentors why  would you want to well mentoring matters  because great leaders inspire future  leaders and by you sharing your  knowledge in whatever space it is  whether it's research or  ops and great talk Kyle that's somebody  you'd want to work for if you were a  devops engineer and the impact of  mentoring has an unusual impact on  diversity and inclusion Harvard Business  Review did some studies earlier this  summer and the title was diversity most  programs don't work here's what to do  about it well that's pretty exciting as  we try and figure out how to make our  communities more diverse and more  inclusive mentoring was one of the few  things that did work and worked quite  well like there was a research study  within that that showed that Hispanic  men Asian men black women Hispanic women  and Asian women had between a nine  percent and twenty-four percent increase  in advancement uh pay when they had some  sort of mentorship and those are pretty  powerful numbers and so think of  yourself as that person there may be  mentoring one or two people and those  one or two people go out and mentor one  or two people as well and mentoring  unlike a lot of diversity programs has a  multiplier effect it has the ability to  exponentially change our communities  well okay that's all well and good but  what's in it for me we have people  across all different dynamics in a  spectrum we have somebody who was like  me who had very little self-confidence  to somebody who's very  hockey who knows everything and really  doesn't need to listen to anybody else  well both of those people and everybody  in between need mentoring to some degree  perhaps the cocky I can do it all  student needs to realize that maybe  there's other people on the project that  they would like to work with that maybe  could share and collaborate with them  and really what that gives us is an  inclusive community to solve real-world  problems one of the things that drew me  to Jupiter was the impact that the  notebooks could have on people  collaborating in order to solve  real-world problems as an example folks  in the Philippines tuberculosis is an  issue over there and getting folks that  have acquired tuberculosis to finish a  drug regimen and the drug regimens not  that expensive but it's prohibitive  enough that over the course of time  people drop the regimen because I think  it takes about six months that could be  wrong so the notebooks enabled  government officials economists  biologists statisticians doctors to come  together and have sort of a common  language if you will it reduced some of  the jargon of each individual problem  space because you could visualize some  of the data later on and then at the  same time it gave people a sort of a  ramp up the learning curve about the  other disciplines so that they were able  to get an idea into a process and trial  faster and so  when we look at the problems that are  really facing us in the world most of  those problems are not going to be  solved by one person that was actually  my mom in her later stages of alzheimers  and for anybody who's taken care of an  elderly parent there are lots of things  to maneuver good care good resources  financial payments for things the  humanity of the person that you are  caring for and those problems are not  going to be solved by maybe as much as I  would like as one computer software  developer I'm not going to solve that  problem but if I have a diverse  community with lots of strengths and  different experiences somebody can solve  it some group of people can solve it and  think about the problem spaces that you  work in there are likely ways that you  can better communicate with research  partners with other groups within your  organization and it matters because the  hard problems are the ones that are most  worth solving or at least moving forward  on because it's possible to find a  solution so how do you mentor I tend to  look at mentors as not the big huge  formal like I'm going to have this  person be my mentor for the next five  years as I move up the ranks of  Corporate America that's great and that  does work as well but I in our busy  lives and you know the different roles  that we play researcher developer parent  spouse daughter son a Little League  coach chess  somebody I met yesterday he was teaching  chess to elementary schools it was  really cool and another gentleman had a  daughter that was in an outreach program  to learn violin in Durham which I think  has been pretty successful so how do you  mentor well it bad acronym I'm sorry but  basically be a slacker is the best way  to mentor so what you can do in 5  minutes 10 minutes half an hour an hour  matters matters a lot because i'll say  margaret didn't spend more than an hour  with me at any given point in time so  there's five things in this slacker  acronym the first one is show up because  if you don't show up nothing is going to  happen so that's step one kind of ground  zero so be open to people approaching  you and at a scifi conference a couple  of years ago maybe three now I said well  it's just common courtesy just say hello  to people and I had a whole room of  folks including my college daughter  laughing at me like uh we're developers  we can't do that and I was like okay aim  a little higher you can do this we all  like the same things at least when it  comes to code the next thing is to  listen you won't know what a person  needs unless you listen to them I'm not  going to tell you that I don't have  enough money for a winter coat in Boston  if you haven't set the stage and  listened to me before you won't know  that I need help on installing Jupiter  hub on my cluster in my supercomputer  center  if you're not listening to me and along  with that is asking questions and not  the kind of questions that sort of  sometimes show how much we know but  asking the questions like that are more  open-ended what do you think about that  why do you think that would be helpful I  don't really know your problem space as  well as you do are there a few ideas  that you think might work maybe we could  talk about them and then part of being  that slacker is to get off the couch and  actually act on something and the  actions can be small or they can be  larger because let's face it life is  kind of a funny thing it evolves over  time sometimes we have time sometimes we  don't but if you say you're going to do  something try and do it or own up to the  fact that you can't do it and apologize  and say sorry I thought I could but I  can't but perhaps so-and-so could help  you that matters it's it's for building  trust both individually and trust in a  larger space of a community and then the  last part of being a slacker is what  slackers really like which is rest  recharge it is really important to take  care of yourself and it's something that  we don't always do well as developers we  want to stay up all night coating on the  really cool thing we want to contribute  to all these different open source  projects but if we aren't taking care of  ourselves we're liable to get more angry  more short with people unhealthy so it  is worth recharge  you can't be a good mentor unless you  take care of yourself so being a slacker  show up listen question act recharge and  the question I'm going to leave you is  what will you do to encourage others to  imagine the possible thank you
 

